> Thanks for your effort, la korbi, but I'm afraid your definition is wrong=
! :(
>
> What's indicated by "be'u" isn't a lack of an emotion, but that the
> emotion is caused by or has to do with lacking something else. =A0For
> instance ".oi be'u" means not a lack of pain, but rather pain because
> of the lack of something.
>
> Here's the relevant part of the CLL:
>
>
> *******
> *******
>
> The cmavo ``be'u'' expresses, roughly speaking, whether the emotion it
> modifies is in response to something you don't have enough of,
> something you have enough of, or something you have too much of. It is
> more or less the attitudinal equivalent of the subjective quantifier
> cmavo ``mo'a'', ``rau'', and ``du'e'' (these belong to selma'o PA, and
> are discussed in Chapter 18). For example,
>
> 7.8) =A0.uiro'obe'unai
> =A0 =A0[Yay!] [physical] [Enough!]
>
> might be something you say after a large meal which you enjoyed.
>
> Like all modifiers, ``be'u'' can be used alone:
>
> 7.9) =A0le cukta be'u cu zvati ma
> =A0 =A0The book [Needed!] is at-location [what sumti?]
> =A0 =A0Where's the book? --- I need it!
>
